  <section>
    <h2>Abstract</h2>
    <p>
      Conditional memory expands language-model capacity through selective lookup, but the
      full embedding table must remain available for every possible input. We introduce
      <strong>Conditional Program (CP)</strong>, a compressed representation that
      reconstructs only the rows an input requests. CP represents each row by a short code
      and a local scale, with a reconstruction program shared across the table. We
      instantiate this representation with a Gaussian trellis, norm restoration and
      independently decodable row records. CP's two-bit code lowers output KL by 41–63%
      relative to 2-bit round-to-nearest. Analysis across three trained n-gram memory
      families shows that Gaussian-like coordinate statistics coexist with learned
      relationships among rows; at matched storage budgets, the shared trellis outperforms
      the fitted codebooks and low-rank residual variants we test. For DeepSeek, it reduces
      table storage from 203 GB to 51 GB, leaving room for larger workloads when the table
      is kept on GPU.
    </p>
  </section>

