  <section>
    <h2>1. The problem: a huge table, read a few rows at a time</h2>
    <p>
      Conditional memory adds capacity without a proportional increase in per-token
      compute: a model hashes input n-grams into row addresses and reads only those rows
      from a large embedding table. DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash, Qwen3.8-Flash-Next and
      LongCat-Flash-Lite all carry such tables — 202.7 GB, 102.4 GB and 62.8 GB
      respectively. Moving them from GPU to host memory changes where that cost is paid
      without reducing it.
    </p>
    <p>
      Weight quantization methods are built for dense matrices. A lookup table is
      different: storage must shrink across the <em>whole</em> table, but each request
      touches only a handful of rows. A format that couples rows inside larger tiles
      saves bytes yet forces the runtime to fetch and decode unrelated data on every
      lookup.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>2. Conditional Program</h2>
    <p>
      CP stores each row <em>e<sub>i</sub></em> as a short code <em>c<sub>i</sub></em> and a
      local scale <em>α<sub>i</sub></em>, and reconstructs it with a decoder shared by the
      whole table: <em>ê<sub>i</sub></em> = <em>α<sub>i</sub></em> · <em>f</em>(<em>c<sub>i</sub></em>).
      Three design choices follow from the access pattern and the row statistics.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Shared Gaussian trellis.</strong> Normalized rows accumulate many small,
        weakly dependent training contributions, so their coordinates look approximately
        Gaussian. CP encodes them in their stored coordinates with a Gaussian HYB bitshift
        trellis (tail-biting), without an extra rotation.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Norm restoration.</strong> Instead of the least-squares scale, which
        shrinks rows whose direction is imperfectly decoded, CP sets
        <em>α<sub>i</sub></em> = ‖<em>e<sub>i</sub></em>‖ / ‖<em>f</em>(<em>c<sub>i</sub></em>)‖,
        preserving the magnitude the model consumes.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Independent row records.</strong> Each code and its bf16 scale form one
        addressable record. A fused kernel fetches and decodes only the requested rows
        and hands them to the unchanged readout in the original lookup order.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
