  <section>
    <h2>4. Output fidelity across storage budgets</h2>
    <figure>
      <img
        src="/ngram-memories/img/kl_frontier.png"
        alt="Table size versus output KL on DeepSeek, Qwen and LongCat; the CP curve lies below and left of RTN, QTIP and four-bit float formats"
        loading="lazy"
      />
      <figcaption>
        Table size versus six-domain geometric-mean output KL (log scale); lower and
        further left is better. CP labels include per-row scales.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p>
      Task scores can hide behavioral drift, so we also measure per-token
      KL(<em>p</em><sub>orig</sub> ‖ <em>p</em><sub>quant</sub>) over six text domains. At
      two bits CP lowers KL by <strong>41–63%</strong> relative to RTN and by a further
      3–9% relative to a QTIP-style trellis with one scale per table. CP at 2.5 bits
      approaches RTN 3-bit with less storage, and at higher rates it beats NVFP4 and
      MXFP4 while using less memory.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>5. What matters in the design</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>A shared trellis beats fitted codebooks.</strong> Frequently read rows do
      share structure — 16 fitted directions capture 10–33% of their energy against a
      6–10% Gaussian reference. Yet at a matched 2.10 bpp on Qwen, the shared trellis
      (KL 0.0198) outperforms vector quantizers fitted to the table (0.0226–0.0238), and
      explicit mean or principal-component terms buy little for the storage they cost.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Restore the norm, not the least-squares fit.</strong> With identical codes
      and storage, the norm-restoring scale gives lower output KL than the
      least-squares scale on all three models (e.g. 0.0327 vs. 0.0349 on Qwen, 0.0426
      vs. 0.0462 on LongCat). Minimizing reconstruction error is not the same as
      preserving model behavior. Within-row rotation shows no resolved benefit.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>6. Serving</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>Selective lookup.</strong> Replaying 4,096-token DeepSeek lookups on one
      H200, CP's row records complete in 0.190 ms from HBM versus 0.221 ms for a
      tile-window decoder over QTIP's tiled layout, and in <strong>6.54 ms versus
      10.78 ms</strong> from pinned host DRAM over PCIe.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Workload capacity.</strong> On four H200s, the DeepSeek table drops from
      50.7 GB to 12.7 GB per GPU. Prefill for 8 × 4k tokens falls from 64.66 s to 13.27 s,
      and a 512-sequence decode batch and a 64k-token prefill that run out of memory
      with FP8 tables now fit. On eight GPUs, CP frees 19.0 GB per GPU, raising the
      largest tested decode batch from 1,024 to at least 2,048 and prefill from 96k to
      at least 128k tokens.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Scope.</strong> Results cover post-training compression of three models
      with hashed lookup tables and may not extend to other memory architectures. When
      GPU memory is already sufficient, complete inference steps show little speed
      improvement in our implementation.
    </p>
  </section>

